Switch interfaces do not have the "shutdown" command applied by default (as opposed to routers) which will result in up/up state if connected to another device, or 
down/down state if not connected.

Show interfaces status to see the different interfaces of the switch

Half duplex: device cannot send and receive data at the same time. It has to wait for the end of the other operation
Full duplex: can both send and receive at the same time

A hub is a device that will broadcast every message to all devices connected to it (it's considered layer 1). There is a high risk of collision using switch. To mitigate this, the devices 
connected to the hub can do Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection (CSMA-CD). Basically, before sending frames, devices listen until they detect that 
other devices are not sending. If a collision does happen, the device sends a jamming signal to inform that a collision happened. Each device will wait a random 
period of time before sending a frame again.

Autonegociation should be used on all device to prevent duplex mismatching and collision.

Show interfaces can also show the different errors detected on an interface
Different interface errors: runts -> frames that are smaller than the minimum frame size (64 bytes)
                            giants -> frames that are larger than the maximum frame size (1518 bytes)
                            CRC -> frames that failed the CRC check
                            frame -> frames that have an incorrect format

Autonegociation and autoduplex: if you connect a port configured as auto to a peer device that has auto-negotiation disabled (manually hardcoded speed and duplex), 
the auto port uses a mechanism called parallel detection.The auto port determines its settings using two strict rules:The Speed Rule -> The switch analyzes the 
electrical frequency of the incoming signals on the physical wire. It detects the exact speed (10, 100, or 1000 Mbps) with 100% accuracy, even if the other side
refuses to negotiate.The Duplex Fallback Rule -> Because duplex mode cannot be sensed physically or electrically without communication, Cisco applies standard 
fallback defaults based on the detected speed: If the detected speed is 10 Mbps or 100 Mbps, the port defaults to Half-Duplex. If the detected speed is 1000 Mbps 
(1 Gbps) or higher the port defaults to Full-Duplex (because the Gigabit standard requires full-duplex)
Example: Exam Trap: Cisco frequently tests you on this specific scenario. Imagine two connected switches: Switch A: Manually hardcoded to speed 100 and duplex 
full. (Auto-negotiation is turned off). Switch B: Left at factory defaults (speed auto, duplex auto).What happens according to CCNA rules: Switch B senses the
incoming electrical signals and sets its speed to 100 Mbps. Because Switch A is not sending negotiation pulses, Switch B applies the fallback rule for 100 Mbps 
and sets its duplex to Half-Duplex. The Result: Switch A is in Full, Switch B is in Half. You now have a Duplex Mismatch.
